a collective sigh of relief today as a  strike by cp rail workers comes to an  end this is a very big deal for farmers  but also consumers right across the  country and marianne dumaine joins us  live with the latest details good  afternoon to you marianne so if you  could break down how this all went and  the timeline of it all too  good afternoon candace well i can tell  you they're not going to need these  picket signs behind me here like they  did over the past two days instead three  thousand unionized workers from  engineers conductors and even rail yard  staff are back on the job this hour and  that's including here in the city's east  end where we are right now and that's  after cp rail and the union agreed on  final and binding arbitration early this  morning we know that trains did come to  a halt on sunday as both sides tried to  reach an agreement but this morning they  started slowly moving again teamster's  canada rail conference says binding  arbitration wasn't the preferred method  but it said it managed to negotiate  terms that are in the best interest of  its members members who stopped work and  were out with signs for the past two  days the union says wages and pensions  do remain key stumbling blocks now the  agreement means cp can resume essential  services for the north american supply  chain  there were concerns that if the work  stoppage went any longer it would  severely impact canada's supply chain  and even grocery store shelves here's  more from one expert on what we avoided  if this  labor dispute would have dragged on uh  it would have been quite problematic for  farmers uh in the us uh in asia around  the world to get fertilizers to increase  productivity because this year it's it's  it's going to be quite critical given  what's going on in ukraine there is  going to be a deficit of commodities  including wheat corn barley so we need  all the help we can get  and since we've been here this morning  there have already been some trains that  left the area and you can see this hour  there are some crews there continuing  some work on the tracks as for the union  it says it will not be speaking to the  media until this whole arbitration  process is complete i'll send it back to  you candace okay thanks so much that is  marianne demaine reporting
 

